      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2519.4,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 698,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHowever, water is much more than just a chemical formula. It is the most abundant and essential substance for life on Earth. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, why it matters, and how it behaves:\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Three States of Matter\nWater is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct physical states:\n*   **Liquid:** What we commonly drink, and what fills the oceans, rivers, and lakes.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Found in glaciers, snow, ice cubes, and frost.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Found in the air as humidity, in clouds, and in steam from boiling water.\n\n### 2. Unique Physical Properties\nWater has several unusual scientific properties that make it vital for the universe and life:\n*   **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This is why it can carry nutrients, minerals, and chemicals through our bodies and through the soil.\n*   **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some bugs to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which helps water travel up the roots of tall trees).\n*   **Ice Floats:** Most liquids shrink and become denser when they freeze, but water actually *expands* and becomes less dense. This is why ice floats on water. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, destroying all aquatic life.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This helps regulate the climate of the Earth and keeps living organisms from overheating.\n\n### 3. Water on Earth\n*   **Coverage:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered in water, mostly by oceans.\n*   **Distribution:** \n    *   About **96.5%** of Earth's water is salt water in the oceans.\n    *   Only about **2.5%** is freshwater.\n    *   Most of that freshwater is trapped in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground, leaving less than **1%** easily accessible for human use in lakes and rivers.\n\n### 4. Importance to Life\nWater is the foundation of all known life. \n*   **Biology:** The human body is made up of about **60% water**. It regulates body temperature, lubricates joints, flushes out waste, and protects organs and tissues.\n*   **Ecosystems:** Plants need water to perform photosynthesis (making their own food), and countless animals and plants live entirely in aquatic environments.\n*   **Civilization:** Human history has revolved around water. Cities and agriculture grew up near rivers (like the Nile, Tigris, and Mississippi) because water is required to grow food, cook, and maintain hygiene.\n\nIn short, water is the lifeblood of our planet—a simple molecule with complex and extraordinary properties that make existence possible."
